WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.660
In recent years, high school sports has frequently
been a punching bag for reformers

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.880
who see a distraction and critics
who see a cultural backwater.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.720
Some even blame school sports for sub-par
US performance on international tests.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:22.160
But the evidence suggests that school sports
do much to promote academic success,

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.820
and social development. One influential study found that
high school student athletes got better grades,

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.660
selected more challenging courses,
and had higher levels

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:35.300
of educational attainment — across
socioeconomic status, gender, and race.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:40.940
Another major study linked sports participation
with increases in math and science achievement.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:45.640
Other studies have found that participating in sports
can make students more likely to graduate,

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.100
improve race relations, and boost civic engagement.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:54.540
There are surely times and places when sports clash with a school's academic mission.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:01:00.160
But, as we seek to nurture self-control,
responsibility, and good citizenship,

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:06.000
remember that high school have established
programs with a track record of doing just that.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.960
Do you think high school sports are a good thing for American students? Let us know in our poll.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:16.800
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like
our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:22.080
and be sure to like and subscribe for
more research and videos from AEI.

